<form version="1.1">
  <label>Click Lat/Lng Token Demo</label>
  <init>
    <set token="clickedLat">0</set>
    <set token="clickedLng">0</set>
    <set token="clickedLatLng">Not yet clicked</set>
  </init>
  <fieldset submitButton="false"></fieldset>
  <row>
    <panel>
      <html>
        <h2>Click Lat/Lng Token Feature</h2>
        <p>
          When enabled, clicking anywhere on the map immediately sets three Splunk dashboard tokens —
          <code>$$clickedLat$$</code>, <code>$$clickedLng$$</code>, and <code>$$clickedLatLng$$</code> —
          that can drive searches, filters, and drilldowns elsewhere on the dashboard — no custom
          JavaScript required.
        </p>
        <table style="border-collapse:collapse; margin:10px 0 16px 0;">
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:4px 16px 4px 0; font-family:monospace; font-weight:bold;">$clickedLat$</td>
            <td style="padding:4px 0;">Latitude of the clicked point (e.g. <code>39.7392</code>)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:4px 16px 4px 0; font-family:monospace; font-weight:bold;">$clickedLng$</td>
            <td style="padding:4px 0;">Longitude of the clicked point (e.g. <code>-104.9903</code>)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:4px 16px 4px 0; font-family:monospace; font-weight:bold;">$clickedLatLng$</td>
            <td style="padding:4px 0;">Combined coordinate string (e.g. <code>39.7392,-104.9903</code>)</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <h3 style="margin-top:16px;">Enabling the feature</h3>
        <p>
          Open the visualization format menu (the pencil icon on the map panel) and navigate to the
          <b>Marker Options</b> section. Three settings control this feature:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Click Lat/Lng Token</b> — Enable this to activate token-setting on map click.
            When enabled, the map cursor changes to a crosshair to indicate click mode is active.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Show Click Marker</b> — When enabled, a red crosshair icon is placed on the map at
            the last clicked location and replaced on each subsequent click. Disable to set tokens
            silently without a visual marker.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Lat/Lng Decimal Precision</b> — Controls the number of decimal places in the
            coordinate values (default: 4). Higher precision gives more exact coordinates;
            lower precision gives rounded values suitable for coarse filtering.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="margin-top:16px;">Example use cases</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Radius search</b> — use <code>$$clickedLat$$</code> and <code>$$clickedLng$$</code> in a haversine SPL query to find events within N km of the clicked point (demonstrated below)</li>
          <li><b>Geo-filter</b> — pass coordinates into a lookup or subsearch to filter a table or chart to a specific region</li>
          <li><b>External map link</b> — use <code>$$clickedLatLng$$</code> in a drilldown link to open Google Maps, OpenStreetMap, or any geo tool (demonstrated below)</li>
          <li><b>Dynamic SPL</b> — substitute coordinates directly into search queries: <code>| where haversine(lat, lng, $$clickedLat$$, $$clickedLng$$) &lt; 100</code></li>
          <li><b>Cross-panel context</b> — click a region on the map to update charts, tables, or single-value panels elsewhere on the same dashboard</li>
        </ul>
      </html>
    </panel>
  </row>
  <row>
    <panel>
      <title>Click anywhere on the map to set coordinate tokens</title>
      <viz type="leaflet_maps_app.maps-plus">
        <search>
          <query>| makeresults count=15
| streamstats count as _serial
| eval _serial=_serial-1
| eval latitude=mvindex(split("47.6,34.05,41.8,29.7,33.4,39.9,25.7,44.9,36.1,45.5,32.7,39.7,35.2,30.3,42.3", ","), _serial)
| eval longitude=mvindex(split("-122.3,-118.2,-87.6,-95.3,-112.0,-75.1,-80.4,-93.2,-115.1,-122.6,-117.1,-104.9,-80.8,-97.7,-71.0", ","), _serial)
| eval title=mvindex(split("Seattle,Los Angeles,Chicago,Houston,Phoenix,Philadelphia,Miami,Minneapolis,Las Vegas,Portland,San Diego,Denver,Charlotte,Austin,Boston", ","), _serial)
| eval count=mvindex(split("142,287,513,198,76,334,89,210,55,167,301,423,88,256,378", ","), _serial)
| table latitude, longitude, title, count</query>
          <earliest>-24h@h</earliest>
          <latest>now</latest>
          <sampleRatio>1</sampleRatio>
        </search>
        <option name="drilldown">none</option>
